    "result_text": "All changes are in place and the suite is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change makes `householdId` deterministic from `(lastName, postcode)` and reroutes all household-keyed logic through it.\n\n### `OwnerMapper.householdId` (`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`)\n- Now computes the id as the first **12 hex characters** (6 bytes) of `SHA-256(normalizedLastName + \"|\" + postcode)`, replacing the old `lastName + \"|\" + address` / 8-char form. A `null` postcode contributes the empty string. Because `identityKey` already delegates to `householdId`, it inherits the new derivation automatically.\n\n### `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`)\n- **Household duplicate block:** after the existing telephone+email identity check, the create now computes the candidate's `householdId` and looks for any existing owner sharing it. If one exists and the request did **not** set `sharesHousehold`, it is rejected with **409**. When `sharesHousehold` is set, the create proceeds as a *declared household member* \u2014 `sharesHousehold` now only bypasses the block (the link follows from the deterministic id, not from setting anything).\n- **Household size:** `countHouseholdMembers` was rewritten to count existing owners sharing the computed `householdId` (was: normalized last name + address).\n- **Possible-duplicate flag:** a declared member is explicitly **not** flagged (`possibleDuplicate = false`, `possibleDuplicateOf = null`); non-declared creates keep the previous soft-match behavior.\n- Removed the now-unused `normalizeHouseholdKey` helper.\n\n### `openapi.yml`\n- Updated the `householdId` and `sharesHousehold` descriptions/example to match the new `(lastName, postcode)` derivation and the \"declared member, not flagged\" semantics.\n\n### Verification\n- `./mvnw test` \u2192 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0` \u2014 `coreSameLastNameAndPostcodeShareHouseholdId` passes: same last name + postcode share an id (via `sharesHousehold` bypass), a different last name gets a different id.\n\nOne consequential note for your awareness: since the soft-duplicate condition (same last name + postcode, different phone) is now identical to the household key, any such owner is either blocked as a household duplicate (409) or created as a declared member (explicitly not flagged) \u2014 so in practice `possibleDuplicate` is never set to `true`. That's the direct logical consequence of the spec (\"a declared member is not a suspected duplicate\"); I kept the soft-match computation intact for the non-declared path rather than deleting it.",
